    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The scrolls pushed the oldest known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> biblical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> back more than a millennium and showed both the stability of the Masoretic tradition and a fluid text before about 100 CE.</li><li>About 40% are copies of canonical scriptures, about 30% other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a> texts such as Enoch and Jubilees, and the rest sectarian works such as the Community Rule.</li><li>Who wrote them is debated: the Qumran–Essene theory held almost universally until the 1990s is now challenged by Sadducee, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and other origins.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Temple Scroll from Cave 11, by far the longest, measures 8.15 metres, and its original length may have been over 8.75 metres.</p></aside>
    </section>

